member.summary:
Value factor inputs the code computes (08 §4), each 0–1 unless noted, read from the perspective of the side being
valued: const (1); trade_dep (the receiver's exports to the giver as a share of its GDP, scaled so 25%
is 1); trade_dep_giver (the same, the other way); energy_import (the side's energy import share);
oil_above_breakeven (how far oil is above the side's fiscal breakeven, −1..1); gas_price_pain (how far
the side's gas price is above its start, 0–1 at +50%); default_risk and market_stress (the receiver's);
exposure (the giver's holdings of the receiver's debt, scaled so $1 trillion is 1); threat (the side's
highest threat perception of anyone but the other side); tariffed, sanctioned, export_banned
(1 if the giver has that measure on the receiver); opinion (the side's opinion of the other, −1..1);
approval_low (1 when the side's leader is below 40% approval); trait:<name>;
pressure:<tag> (1 if the side has that domestic pressure); goal:<goal id> (its leader's goal
weight).

What the player can see and how intelligence works (09): data/intel/intel.json. Visibility rules say which
variables are directly observable, which are only known through reports (perceived) and which are never shown (hidden);
targets set how hard each foreign country is to read; params tune estimators, whip counts and reports.

