A parsed line of dialogue text (10 §2): literal text, variable slots {name}, word buckets {bucket:variable}
and inline variation [[option A|option B]] (options may hold slots; variations don't nest). Translations (28) may
also inflect by a variable: {g:who|m|f|n|pl} picks the form for its grammatical gender, {n:count|one|count|other}
the form for its number (Bulgarian's count form after numerals), {def:office} its name with the definite article.
Immutable.

member.remarks:
Tick pipeline (02 §3): 1. apply due commands; 2. block if a due decision is unresolved; 3–4. run systems in
 with the due cadences and dated items; 5. modifiers, ledgers and history roll over;
6. advance the tick and date.

member.summary:
Multi-step NPC agendas (07 §6). Weekly, actors whose start trigger holds decide whether to begin (dp.agenda_step);
on each step's day they decide again (proceed, wait or abandon). Steps apply consequences and may become acts others react
to. One agenda per actor, at most  per country, a cooldown per actor and agenda.

            Domestic pressures on a leader (08 §2): what they must answer to at home, as tags. Authored and scheduled for light
            countries (diplomacy.json pressures, e.g. an election on a date), derived from the simulation where it exists:
            

economy_weak: real growth below 1% or an output gap below −1.5;

inflation_high: inflation above 5%;

dependent_on_<code>_market: exports to that country above 10% of GDP;

energy_import_dependent: half or more of its energy imported;

fiscal_needs_high_oil: an oil producer with oil below its fiscal breakeven;

full countries with politics: elite_split (leader security below 40) and unpopular_leader (approval below 38).

Trade disputes (08, replacing 04's automatic retaliation): when a country's goods are hit with tariffs, its leader answers with dp.trade_retaliation after a couple of weeks:
Retaliate: the same tariff on the imposer's goods two weeks later (04 lifts it when the original goes);
Negotiate: propose talks, tariffs lifted in exchange for something the imposer values; if the imposer turns them down or lets them lapse, the leader decides again, now more inclined to retaliate;
Absorb: take the hit, for now (a higher tariff reopens the question).
Inputs: how much the tariff hurts (exports to the imposer as a share of GDP, the rate), how much the leader needs the imposer for security, leverage (dependence each way), domestic pressure, opinion, an alliance, failed talks, and pride, hawkishness, pragmatism and patience.

True if  is a rate-decision day. With 8 meetings a year the calendar follows the FOMC's usual
pattern (late January, mid-March, early May, mid-June, late July, mid-September, early November, mid-December);
other counts space meetings evenly, on the third Wednesday.

Seats that would back the government minus a majority of all seats: positive means it survives a confidence vote
today. A bloc of the government's party backs it unless its support has fallen below ;
another party's bloc only above .

Regime indicators and government drift (05 §9). Six indicators (0–100) move with norm-breaking acts and slowly recover;
their mean sets the regime type through thresholds with hysteresis. The regime type scales power center weights, drives
23's fear input and is what regime_type conditions see.
